{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfdf5qruidc2ug9pfpo2v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I couldn't make any of the four A01 checks fail, so I wrote `verdict.json` with an overall verdict of **upheld**. I only read files and never ran anything.\n\n- **A01.1 holds.** The agent uses its own service account, `clinic-agent`. The IAM role also trusts `clinic-agent-reminders`, but that is a CronJob running the same agent image, not an unrelated service.\n- **A01.2 holds.** The eligibility client exchanges the agent's workload token. The EHR and payments clients use per-clinic secrets from Secrets Manager. Nothing forwards a user's `Authorization` header or cookie.\n- **A01.3 holds.** The user travels as a separate `subject` field on the run context, and the patient is carried in the exchange scope. The agent's own identity stays distinct. The `subject_token` in the exchange is the agent's workload token, not the user's, and there is no `actor_token`. I counted the separate subject field as enough, since the check allows \"a separate delegated-subject field\".\n- **A01.4 holds.** Permissions sit on the `clinic-agent-runtime` role, which only the agent service accounts can assume. Users never get AWS credentials, so removing the role doesn't affect them.\n\nThe closest call was the payments restricted key. It is a static per-clinic key, but only the agent uses it, so I didn't count it as a shared static key. If you read \"static key\" more strictly, A01.2 would flip to fails."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1281,"cache_read_input_tokens":23519,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1281},"output_tokens":480,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":nul